four layings of the thread's letters (hRdIs, half, hb₀, hKp) and two ties on the Script (hMask,
hc). Of the two ties no proof is known here: a program's search over the theorems of the files that
lie with this one found none that ends in `CellIsList`, and `K4MaskTieAt` ends one alone,
`k4MaskTie_iff_at` (Law/ScriptAt.lean), which says that two spellings are one. BESIDE hW: hRdIs,
Lh, hQ, half, fits, hb₀, hKp, hx, hMask, hL, hc, hiW, hTokP: each is one of k4_exchange_tenth's, to
the letter; its head says of each what it asks. hc (`CellIsList`, Law/W/CellsTieOf.lean): the
honest cells against the list that the library's reader makes of the witness; it forces that list
to stand still inside the sizes. `matsAre_head` says that two of the library's readers of one
witness read one thing, 0 outside every size on both sides: nothing on what a cell is worth or on
an honest prover's cells; its head says on which statements under Johnson/Goal/ it rests. WHETHER
ALL THE HYPOTHESES CAN STAND TOGETHER AT AN HONEST SCRIPT HAS NOT BEEN TESTED: no example is known,
and nothing here was evaluated. THAT THE THREAD HOLDS AT ANY RUN, OR THAT ANY STEP OF THE MEMBER
ACCEPTS: the conclusion speaks only where `ThrS` stands and a step accepts; where none does, it
holds emptily. NOT COMPLETENESS. h: the library's hypothesis `P5J.AtMgA`
(Johnson/P5J/MgAtAll.lean); the side library AtFullSide proves, with no hypothesis, that h has a
member at one place (AtFullSide.Top.headMember_zFull, AtFullSide/Top/HeadMemberAtFull.lean, whose
